 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A logarithm is an index: log_b x = n means bⁿ = x. So log₂ 8 = 3 because 2³ = 8.\nlog without a base means base 10: log 1000 = 3.\n• log_b b = 1 and log_b 1 = 0.\nLaws (same base):\n• log a + log b = log (ab)\n• log a − log b = log (a ÷ b)\n• log (aⁿ) = n log a\nExample: log 2 + log 5 = log 10 = 1.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "learn": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "sections": [
   {
    "heading": "A logarithm is an index",
    "text": "We know {pw(2, 3)} = {8}. The logarithm turns this round: log₂ {8} = {3}, read \"log to base {2} of {8} is {3}\".\nlog_b x asks: what power of b gives x?\n• log₃ {81} = {4} because {pw(3, 4)} = {81};\n• log₅ {1} = {0} because {5}{sup(0)} = {1};\n• log b to base b is always {1}.\nWithout a base written, log means base {10}: log {1000} = {3}."
   },
   {
    "heading": "The laws come from the index laws",
    "text": "Since multiplying powers adds indices, logs turn multiplication into addition:\n• log a + log b = log (ab)\n• log a − log b = log (a ÷ b)\n• log aⁿ = n log a\nExample: log {4} + log {25} = log {100} = {2}.\nBefore calculators, people used log tables and these laws to multiply large numbers by adding."
   },
   {
    "heading": "Using a known value",
    "text": "If you know log {2} ≈ {'0.3010'}, you can find others:\n• log {8} = log {pw(2, 3)} = {3} log {2} ≈ {0.9030}\n• log {5} = log ({10} ÷ {2}) = {1} − {0.3010} = {0.6990}\nCareful: log (a + b) is not log a + log b. log ({2} + {8}) = log {10} = {1}, but log {2} + log {8} = log {16}."
   }
  ],
  "key_words": [
   {
    "word": "logarithm",
    "meaning": "log_b x is the power of b that gives x"
   },
   {
    "word": "base",
    "meaning": "the number being raised to the power"
   },
   {
    "word": "common logarithm",
    "meaning": "log to base {10}, written log"
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   "log₂ {64} = {6}",
   "log {0.1} = {-1}",
   "log {3} + log {4} = log {12}",
   "log {pw(5, 3)} = {3} log {5}"
  ],
  "mistake": {
   "wrong": "log {3} + log {4} = log {7}",
   "right": "log {3} + log {4} = log {12}",
   "why": "Adding logarithms multiplies the numbers inside."
  }
 },
 "worked_example": {
